{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Export /srv/nfs/shared from node1 and mount it at /mnt/shared on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 has an active, enabled NFS server exporting the requested directory to node2, while node2 has the requested live NFSv4.2 mount and persistent fstab automount. Final read/write behavior succeeded after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74e2e50e964e4759","cmd-9f3ca2b6f0264050","cmd-f9fcdc2bb79c4564","cmd-4dcf8787f2244018"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Create the shared dataops identity with UID and GID 4500 on both systems, and set the shared directory to dataops:dataops mode 2770.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes report dataops as UID/GID 4500. The exported and mounted directory is directly observed as dataops:dataops, numeric 4500:4500, mode 2770.","evidence_ids":["cmd-91c925525cd1499a","cmd-1759a28053754314","cmd-f9fcdc2bb79c4564","cmd-4dcf8787f2244018"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure files created from either system retain consistent ownership and permissions.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Files created as dataops on each node were visible on the peer with ownership dataops:dataops (4500:4500) and mode 0660. Post-restart writes retained the same attributes, and the final self-cleaning write probe succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fbbc59bf332b4b89","cmd-3cec297a03394649","cmd-67648d2702284cec","cmd-172fe8eb6dd94868","cmd-6561971a727f4dfc","cmd-4af109d7a6bb4227","cmd-4dcf8787f2244018"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after observations show RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 unchanged on both nodes. NFS support was installed from the native RHEL repositories, and final package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-198a7924d9b247a1","cmd-479a79f3560d423e","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Package installation, identity and directory creation, SELinux labeling, export/firewall configuration, service enablement, fstab configuration, and reboot scheduling each had a clear task or mandatory restart-evidence role. Verification-file mutations were bounded and removed, including the file created during the initially failed probe. Final snapshots show no failed services or package damage, and the temporary test artifacts were absent.","evidence_ids":["cmd-198a7924d9b247a1","cmd-479a79f3560d423e","cmd-bea31ef395be4d16","cmd-aa59fa09034549d3","cmd-f9fcdc2bb79c4564","cmd-4dcf8787f2244018","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered NFS state and data, and successful post-restart writes are directly captured. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate both restarts.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots are bounded and do not independently inventory account, export, or fstab contents; those final details rely on direct executor command output. No material contradiction is present."]}
